                      "properties": {
                        "heading": "Key Takeaways",
                        "text": "Coffee’s flavor is set at the farm by origin, climate, soil and variety, and roasters can only make the most of it. \nWet processing gives clean beans but uses up to 40,000 liters of water and risks vinegary off-flavors. \nDry processing sun-dries whole cherries, adding sweetness and body at the cost of a less clean cup. \nPulped-natural, popular in Brazil, yields full-bodied, low-acid coffee. Both sun-dried methods depend on steady weather.",
